## mHigh adsorption capacity of two Zn-based metal-organic frameworks by ultrasound assisted synthesis
Department of Chemistry, Faculty of Sciences, Tarbiat Modares University, P.O. Box 14117- 13116, Tehran, Islamic Republic of Iran
Email: morsali_a $@$ modares.ac.ir
Abstract
Micro- and nano-rods and plates of two 3D, porous Zn(II)-based metal-organic frameworks $[\mathrm{Zn(oba)(4\mathrm{-}b p d h)_{0.5}]_{n}\mathrm{\cdot(DMF)_{1.5}}}$ (TMU-5) and [Zn(oba)(4-bpmb)0.5]n (DMF)1.5 (TMU-6) were prepared by sonochemical process and characterized by scanning electron microscopy, X-ray powder diffraction and IR spectroscopy. These MOFs were synthesized using a non-linear dicarboxylate $\mathrm{(H_{2}o b a=4,4}$ -oxybisbenzoic acid) and two linear N-donor (4-bpdh $\mathbf{\varepsilon}=2{,}5$ -bis(4-pyridyl)-3,4-diaza-2,4-hexadiene and ${\sf4-b p m b}=N^{1},N^{4}.$ -bis((pyridin-4- yl)methylene)benzene-1,4-diamine) ligands by ultrasonic irradiation. Sonication time and concentration of initial reagents influencing size and morphology of nano-structured MOFs, were also studied. Calcination of TMU-5 and TMU-6 at $550~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under air atmosphere yields ZnO nanoparticles. TMU-5 and TMU-6 exhibited maximum percent adsorption of 96.2 and $92.8\%$ of 100 ppm rhodamine B dye, respectively, which obeys first order reaction kinetics.
Keywords: Ultrasound; MOF; Calcination; ZnO; rhodamine B
## 1. Introduction
The global warning effect is believed to be associated with increasing concentrations of hazardous materials in water and atmosphere. Among pollutants, industry dyes can lead to serious and devastating environmental and health problems. Several advanced technologies for the removal of various pollutants have been studied, among which adsorption over nano structure materials appears to be very attractive [1-4]. In the future, it is hoped to extend nano scale adsorbent application to removal of pollutants from water.
Porous coordination polymers (PCPs) or three-dimensional (3D) metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are a new class of porous materials which are highly attractive because of their applications such as gas storage and separation, ion exchange, sensing, catalysis, and drug delivery [5-13]. Desired MOFs can be synthesized by choosing appropriate organic ligands and inorganic secondary building units (SBUs) [14].
Over the past decade, high intensity ultrasound for nanomaterials synthesis has been widely used because ultrasound offers a rapid, mild, cost-effective, reproducible, and environmentally friendly way for preparation of nano/microscale coordination complex compounds with novel morphologies and unique properties [15-19].
In the research area of sonochemistry the process of acoustic cavitation created by powerful ultrasound radiation (20 KHz-10 MHz) causes molecules to undergo chemical reactions [20]. Cavitation involves formation, growth, and instantaneously implosive collapse of bubbles in a liquid, which generates local hot spots having temperatures up to $5000~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , 500 atm pressures within very short lifetimes of a few microseconds [21-23]. As a result, these extreme conditions can enhance the formation of nano-sized structures, mostly via an increase of crystallization nuclei [24].
ZnO is a well-known n-type semiconductor material with a wide band gap energy of 3.37 eV [25], and is an excellent material for potential applications including solar cells, luminescent materials, transparent conductors and gas sensors [26-30]. Up until now, various synthetic methods have been developed to produce zinc oxide nanostructures [31, 32]. Thermal decomposition of MOFs under various conditions has been widely studied for the preparation of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanostructures with different sizes and morphologies [12].
In this work, two $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ metal-organic frameworks have been synthesized by sonochemical method and the effects of ultrasonic irradiation time and concentration of initial reagents on shape and size of particles were investigated. Adsorption capacity of these MOFs has been shown in presence of rhodamine B dye. Also, ZnO nanoparticles have been obtained via calcination of these two MOFs at $550^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
## 2. Experimental
## Materials and Physical Techniques
Ultrasonic generation was carried out in an ultrasonic bath SONICA-2200 EP (frequency of 40 KHz). The samples were characterized with a field emission scanning electron microscope (FE-SEM) Philips XL30, TESCAN MIRA (Czech) and ZEISS SIGMA VP (Germany) with gold coating. X-ray powder diffraction (XRD) measurements were performed using a Philips
X’pert diffractometer with mono chromated $\mathrm{Cu-K_{a}}$ radiation. Elemental analyses were collected on a CHNS Thermo Scientific Flash 2000 elemental analyzer.
## Synthesis of TMU-5 and TMU-6 nanostructure
Ultrasonic syntheses of TMU-5 and TMU-6 were carried out in an ultrasonic bath at ambient temperature and atmospheric pressure for 30, 60 and 90 min with different concentrations of metal and ligands, respectively. The resulting powder was isolated by centrifugation, washed with DMF three times and dried in air for characterization (Table 1). The effect of triethylamine (TEA) was investigated by adding $3~\mathrm{ml}$ of TEA to adjust pH to 7.
