## 3. Results and discussion
The structure of $[\mathrm{Zn(oba)(4\mathrm{-}b p d h)_{0.5}}]_{\mathrm{n}}{\cdot}(\mathrm{DMF)_{y}}$ (TMU-5) consists of 3D, interconnected, narrow pores which is built up from a $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{oba})_{4}$ paddle-wheel secondary building unit (SBU) (Fig. 1) [34]. Threefold interpenetrated structure of $[\mathrm{Zn(oba)(4\mathrm{-}b p m b)_{0.5}]_{n}.(D M F)_{z}}$ (TMU-6) has been obtained through connecting 2D sheets by linear 4-bpmb ligands. These sheets are formed by dicarboxylate oba ligands and $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ centres (Fig. 2). TMU-6 is also based on a binuclear $Z\mathrm{n(II)}$ unit (Fig. 2a) and shows 1D, large pores running along the [101] direction (Fig. 2d) [35].
The morphology and size of TMU-5 and TMU-6 were investigated using scanning electron microscopy (SEM) by changing two parameters; sonication time and concentration of starting materials as well as control of nucleation. For TMU-5, sonication time as a parameter was changed at a constant concentration (Table 1). In 30 and $60\ \mathrm{min}$ , microspheres have been obtained which are constructed from nanorods, whereas in $90~\mathrm{\min}$ duration, separate and uniform nanorods of TMU-5 have been formed (Fig. 3). Hence, sonication time of $90\ \mathrm{min}$ is considered as the optimized value. After this in order to investigate the role of concentration of initial reagents on the morphology and size of product, reactions were performed with two different concentrations (twice and half of the first concentration, Table 1). The results show that high concentrations of initial reagents increased particle size and lead to non-uniform distribution of particle size (Fig. 4a). Contrariwise lower concentration of initial reagents reduced the size of nanorods (Fig. 4b). After this, increasing the speed of nucleation during the synthesis of TMU-5 was tested by adding triethylamine (TEA). Using TEA causes fast nucleation of product due to deprotonated oba ligand and faster nucleation reduces particle size. By this mechanism nanorods with a narrow size distribution and reduced aspect ratio of length to width were obtained (Fig. 4c) [24].
A similar process was used for TMU-6 on similar concentrations of initial reagents. In 30, 60 and 90 min of duration, microrod, nanoplate and microplate morphologies have been obtained, respectively (Fig. 5). Additionally, in experiments of 60 min duration, concentrations were changed to half and twice of the initial concentration. The results show that similar to TMU-5 high concentrations of initial reagents increased plate size and lead to non-uniform distribution while lower concentration of initial reagents reduced the size of nanoplates (Fig. 6a and b). Then, increasing speed of nucleation during the synthesis of TMU-6 was tested by adding TEA to reaction with conditions of $\mathrm{\Delta[oba]=[Zn(OAc)_{2}.4H_{2}O]=}$ [0.025] M and $[4\mathrm{-bpmb}]=[0.0125]$ and $30~\mathrm{min}$ . FE-SEM images show that fast nucleation and slower growth reduced size of the rods from micro to nano (Fig. 6c).
The IR spectra of both crystals and nanorods of TMU-5 and TMU-6 produced by conventional heating and sonochemical method show the symmetric $\upnu_{\mathrm{sym}}(\mathrm{COO})$ and asymmetric $\upnu_{\mathrm{as}}(\mathbf{COO})$ vibrations of the carboxylate groups around $1400~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ and $1600~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , respectively. Also the characteristic absorption peak $(\mathrm{v_{C=O}}=1679\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1},$ ) of DMF is observed in IR spectra of these two MOFs (Fig. 7).
A comparison between powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of the simulated (derived from the single crystal structure of TMU-5 and TMU-6) and experimental (resulting from the sonochemical process) confirms that the sonochemically synthesized TMU-5 and TMU-6 are structurally identical to those prepared through conventional heating (Fig. 8).
To prepare zinc oxide nanoparticles, TMU-5 and TMU-6 were calcined at $550~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{~h~}$ .
Fig. 9 depicts XRD patterns of the residue obtained from calcination of these MOFs. The
Bragg diffraction peaks exhibit the typical patterns of hexagonal wurtzite structure of ZnO consistent with the reported data by the JCPDS card number 36-1451 with lattice parameters of $a=3.25$ and $c=5.20\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . The SEM images of the residues obtained from direct calcination of TMU-5 and TMU-6 show the formation of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanoparticles (Fig. 10).
## Evaluation of adsorption capacity
Adsorption capacity of TMU-5 and TMU-6 nanostructures with different morphologies and ZnO nanoparticles obtained from thermolysis of them in presence of 100 ppm RhB aqueous solution after $90~\mathrm{min}$ was investigated (Fig. 11). The maximum percent adsorption of RhB is roughly 96.2 and $92.8\%$ after $90~\mathrm{{min}}$ in presence of 5F and 6F, respectively (Fig. X11 a and b). As shown in Fig 11a, a decrease in particle size together with uniform morphology can cause more adsorption of target pollutant. Moreover, ZnO nanoparticles prepared by thermolysis of MOFs can decrease the RhB concentration up to $68.6\%$ at this time, while in cases of other morphologies of MOFs it takes place with a lower percentage of adsorption. On the other hand, there is a large change in the RhB concentration in a shorter time with the nanorod morphology in both MOFs (5F and 6F) (Fig. 11 c and d).
Also, adsorption capacity in the case of TMU-5 nanostructure is little higher than that of TMU-6 (Fig. 11e). This observation may be attributed to basicity of N-donor group in the MOF structure and the adsorption mechanism is based on acid-base interactions as well as the kind of morphology and particle size of adsorbent. A decrease in the particle size (Figs. 3-6), causes an increase in adsorption capacity of sample (Fig. 11). In addition, the adsorption of samples in presence of different concentrations of RhB for $120~\mathrm{{min}}$ was investigated (Fig. 11 e). As it is shown, the removal efficiency decreases with an increase in RhB concentration due to the occupation of MOF pores in higher concentration of dye.
In order to know the kinetics of RhB adsorption in the solutions suspended on 5F and 6F, different kinds of kinetics orders are attempted to express the reaction kinetics (Fig. 12 and Table 2). ${\bf R}_{0}$ , ${\bf R}_{1}$ and ${\bf R}_{2}$ values represent the correlation coefficients of zero, first and second order rate equations, respectively (Table 2) and shows that ${\bf R}_{1}$ has the best correlation for two adsorbents. Therefore, it is suggested that the RhB adsorption in the solutions suspended on
MOFs belongs to first order reaction kinetics. Also, $\mathrm{K}_{1}$ value for 5F is higher than that for 6F, so this MOF has higher capacity for removing RhB dye. Also, the adsorbent of 5F is very stable and is nearly retained during four repeated experiments. The XRD pattern of the adsorbent before and after repeating the reaction four times is shown in Fig. 13 which clearly indicates that the structure remained the same and no change was observed.
